ll wide that leaves the middle of one side. A stick never starts at a corner. Rectangles can overlap, and sticks are drawn over other rectangles. Examples:\n- In pair 1, the 3-rectangle is partly hidden under the 7-rectangle.\n- In pair 2, the 8-stick at row 11 covers two cells of the 2-rectangle.\n\n**What happens.** Each rectangle with a stick slides along the stick's direction. It moves by the stick's length, and the stick disappears. The evidence:\n- **Pair 3, colour 8:** the rectangle is at columns 16–18 with a 4-cell stick pointing left. In the output it is at columns 12–14, so it moved 4 to the left.\n- **Pair 3, colour 2:** the rectangle is at columns 5–7 with a 6-cell stick pointing right. It ends up at columns 11–13.\n- **Pair 1, colour 7:** a 2-cell stick points down, and the rectangle moves from rows 3–6 to rows 5–8.\n- **Pair 1, colour 6:** a 2-cell stick points right, and the rectangle moves from columns 3–8 to columns 5–10.\n- **Pair 2:** colour 5 moves 3 right, and colour 8 moves 4 right.\n\n**What the output shows about layering.**\n- Rectangles without a stick stay where they are, and they are restored to their full size. The 3-rectangle in pair 1 reappears as a complete 5×4 block once the 7-rectangle has left it.\n- Moved rectangles are painted on top of the still ones. In pair 1, the moved 7 covers part of the 1. In pair 2, the moved 8 covers part of the 2.\n\n**Finding the stick in the code.** For each colour I take its bounding box. Then I trim from each side any edge column (or row) that holds exactly one cell of that colour, but only if that cell is not on the corner rows or columns of the box.\n- The corner condition matters. In pair 2, the hidden 3-rectangle shows only a single cell, at its top-left corner, in row 12. That row must not be mistaken for a stick.\n- The number of lines trimmed is the move distance, and the side they were on gives the direction.\n- What remains of the bounding box is the full rectangle.\n\n**Applying it to the test.**\n- Colour 8 has a 5-cell stick pointing left, so it moves from columns 8–11 to columns 3–6. It will be painted over the 2-rectangle.\n- Colour 1 has a 2-cell stick pointing up, so it moves to rows 5–9.\n- Colour 7 has a 2-cell stick pointing up, so it moves to rows 14–16. It will be painted over the bottom of the 3-rectangle.\n- Colours 2 and 3 have no stick. They stay put and are restored to full rectangles first.\n\n## Program","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    still, moving = [], []\n    for c in sorted(set(g.flatten().tolist()) - {bg}):\n        m = (g == c)\n        ys, xs = np.nonzero(m)\n        y0, y1, x0, x1 = ys.min(), ys.max(), xs.min(), xs.max()\n        dy = dx = 0\n        # right\n        while x1 > x0:\n            col = np.nonzero(m[y0:y1+1, x1])[0]\n            if len(col) == 1 and 0 < col[0] < y1 - y0:\n                x1 -= 1; dx += 1\n            else:\n                break\n        while x1 > x0:\n            col = np.nonzero(m[y0:y1+1, x0])[0]\n            if len(col) == 1 and 0 < col[0] < y1 - y0:\n                x0 += 1; dx -= 1\n            else:\n                break\n        while y1 > y0:\n            row = np.nonzero(m[y1, x0:x1+1])[0]\n            if len(row) == 1 and 0 < row[0] < x1 - x0:\n                y1 -= 1; dy += 1\n            else:\n                break\n        while y1 > y0:\n            row = np.nonzero(m[y0, x0:x1+1])[0]\n            if len(row) == 1 and 0 < row[0] < x1 - x0:\n                y0 += 1; dy -= 1\n            else:\n                break\n        rect = (c, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n        if dy or dx:\n            moving.append((rect, dy, dx))\n        else:\n            still.append(rect)\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    for c, y0, x0, y1, x1 in still:\n        out[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1] = c\n    emit(out, \"Restore the rectangles without a stick to their full size, in place.\")\n    for (c, y0, x0, y1, x1), dy, dx in moving:\n        a0, a1 = max(0, y0+dy), min(H-1, y1+dy)\n        b0, b1 = max(0, x0+dx), min(W-1, x1+dx)\n        out[a0:a1+1, b0:b1+1] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Slide rectangle {c} by the length of its stick ({dy},{dx}) and drop the stick.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What happens.** Each rectangle with a stick slides along the stick's direction."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Slide rectangle 6 by the length of its stick (0,2) and drop the stick."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Slide rectangle 7 by the length of its stick (2,0) and drop the stick."}],"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- In pair 1, the 3-rectangle is partly hidden under the 7-rectangle."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Rectangles without a stick stay where they are, and they are restored to their full size."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"They stay put and are restored to full rectangles first."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The number of lines trimmed is the move distance, and the side they were on gives the direction."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Finding the stick in the code.** For each colour I take its bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- What remains of the bounding box is the full rectangle."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Rectangles can overlap, and sticks are drawn over other rectangles."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Moved rectangles are painted on top of the still ones."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"ac093c6385b5a65650570527c55500dc78800c76b02d1346f633111b7e3a5884","task_sha256":"6370f0b97c1a43099ce5fdbf91026c7486da8c9b3d155bb8875ac27ad44398b6","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
